{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1562220-how-do-you-house-rule-horrid-reality-chimerstry-5","id":1562220,"title":"How do you house rule Horrid Reality? (Chimerstry 5)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-07-27T03:45:13-07:00","id":1562220,"title":"How do you house rule Horrid Reality? (Chimerstry 5)","content":"Hello, in V20 how do you rule Horrid reality?<br /> According to the description the Ravnos can make the victim believe the illusion. If the Ravnos want to deal damage a roll is required, but if no damage is dealt, it's automatic.<br /> <br /> Do you set any limits to what the illusion can be or how unbelievably it can be?<br /> <br /> <br /> Let's say the Ravnos uses Horrid Reality to make a voctim believe a cage has formed around them. How would you handle the following situations?<br /> -The victim tries to lockpick their way out of the cage.<br /> -The victim tries to break the cage using a sword or something similar.<br /> -The victim slams their body against the cage wall trying to escape.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11010-ersito","name":"erSito","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11010_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-27T09:47:21-07:00","id":1562227,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure we had one such question not long ago.<br /> <br /> Anyway, you have to keep your question straight: houserule is not how one uses the power/rule, but how one modifies it.<br /> <br /> Do you want to know how people that dislike the rules change them?<br /> How people interpret them?<br /> If houserules for it are common?<br /> Or are you assuming they're unusable as is and houserules are unequivocally needed?<br /> <br /> Those are not the same and shouldn't be conflated. I'll answer assuming the second:<br /> <br /> Horrid Reality doesn't need limits. The illusion is a direct alteration of the target's perception, it's a hallucination. The key point isn't rational belief, but overwhelming sensation.<br /> <br /> Let's say you're a victim and the illusion is that the whole city around you became a lovecraftian tentacled nightmare. You know for a fact this is impossible, right? Well...<br /> <br /> -&gt; All your senses give you distinct, consistent and complete input. You feel it, you smell it, you see and hear not only in general terms, but every detail. The experience is <u><b>complete</b></u>.<br /> <br /> -&gt; The sensations are real. You are perceiving those things regardless if they're real or not. If you conclude this is an illusion, the illusion will remain there. They obstruct perception for real. You are lucid, awake, aware, and still there's no flaw in the experience.<br /> <br /> -&gt; It affects one target and has limited capacity for serious damage. The notion of &quot;any illusion&quot; gives it a sensation of limitless scope, but actually the scope is one person. This is not as much as it seems.<br /> <br /> For your specific questions:<br /> <br /> -The victim tries to lockpick their way out of the cage.<br /> They do exactly that. The victim will experience the interaction as normal. Depending on how the illusion is built, they may even succeed. The fact the instruments aren't interacting with anything won't change how the victim feels the solid mechanism.<br /> <br /> -The victim tries to break the cage using a sword or something similar.<br /> <br /> Same as above. The sword won't interact, but the victim will feel an interaction as part of the illusion. The sword itself may not be real, won't change anything. The attempt can work or not, regardless, the experience fits the result.<br /> <br /> -The victim slams their body against the cage wall trying to escape. <br /> <br /> Once again as above, with a caveat: this one will work. Since the cage isn't real, if the victim truly does that, there's nothing really capable of stopping the body. The experience remain real, though, the victim feels the process of breaking, may even get hurt as per the power effect, and sees the ruined cage.<br /> <br /> Just be aware: a victim under this type of illusion can self-sabotage without noticing, like running for a tackle, but slowing down in the last instant, so what the victim senses they're doing may not fit what they're actually doing. That's on the ST.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}